    {
      "id": "eof-modes-and-pdo-index",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/eof-modes-and-pdo-index.md",
      "sha256": "3544f2f552ecd0f41c41eb09edf7012f49ac8421900f2b571b8498d37f2726b1",
      "reason": "The question computes the PDO as the leading EOF of North Pacific SST anomalies and asks for explained variance, regression amplitude and pattern correlation, all of which this fragment says depend on area weighting, domain, record length and sign convention.",
      "notes_cut": 1,
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Fix the arbitrary EOF sign by the convention that positive index = negative SST anomalies in the central/western North Pacific; a negative pattern correlation is then a real disagreement, not a flip.",
        "The EOF is dimensionless: take the mode's amplitude from the regression pattern (anomalies regressed on the standardised index), whose area-weighted RMS is the strength in degrees; compare explained variance only on the same grid and period."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices.md",
      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The PDO index must be formed from anomalies relative to the 1950-2014 monthly climatology, and this fragment constrains how anomalies, baselines and index comparisons are defined.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Build each model's index against its own 1950-2014 climatology (`compute_anomaly_vs_own_climatology`), each calendar month matched to its own climatological month so the seasonal cycle is removed; state climatology and baseline (`base_period`) in the answer.",
        "An offset between the reconstructed and published index can come from their different baselines (one constant per calendar month) and is not an error; only the variability is comparable."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The pattern correlations between each model regression pattern and the ERSSTv5 regression pattern must be computed only over cells the two sources share, as this fragment requires for a variability mode compared with a reference.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "A variability mode's regression pattern is not masked to other sources' coverage: correlate each model pattern with the reference pattern over the cells the two share (`analyze_correlation` drops cells either lacks) and report the shared cell counts."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell.md",
      "sha256": "4acdeba0f49784d4435dbc4eed34ca9777254313daa951641759fa02df24cf64",
      "reason": "The CMIP6 `tos` used here is top-cell bulk temperature rather than a skin temperature, which affects how it can be compared with ERSSTv5 SST.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "`tos` is the bulk temperature of the top model cell, whose thickness differs between the models (0–10 m CESM2, 0–2 m GFDL-ESM4, 0–12 m MPI-ESM1-2-LR); this limits how exactly amplitudes are comparable.",
        "Sea-ice-covered cells carry under-ice water temperature near freezing and are not missing, so the high-latitude part of the domain includes values near −1.8 °C."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-native-grid-vs-regridded",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-native-grid-vs-regridded.md",
      "sha256": "8ff046ffbadc5794fcd2f8fe93d3bb947c736dec650aa3a3cd6e2412b7da8e68",
      "reason": "The question explicitly regrids by bilinear interpolation onto a 1-degree grid, and this fragment warns that regridding changes masks and conservation and should be limited to maps and pattern comparisons such as these.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Because the EOF mode, its explained variance and its regression pattern depend on the grid, put every source, ERSSTv5 included, on one common grid before the analysis and take all mode numbers there.",
        "The question prescribes regridding \"by bilinear interpolation onto the 1-degree grid\"; the fragment prefers 'conservative' going to a coarser grid and before any area-weighted step, so state bilinear as the method used and that a regridded explained variance/pattern correlation depends on it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "serial-correlation-and-significance",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/serial-correlation-and-significance.md",
      "sha256": "fe11bd008257a589f44e369faba5fa31ab3cf9ad99280e88c763ac45d2b0e917",
      "reason": "The question asks for lag-1-year autocorrelation and correlation with the published PDO series, and this fragment requires accounting for autocorrelation when interpreting correlations and significance.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Comparing two estimates of the same quantity (ERSSTv5-derived index vs the published PDO) needs no detrending: the shared trend is part of the agreement being checked, not a spurious link.",
        "Across space no effective-sample-size correction exists for a pattern correlation: read its coefficient, not its p-value."
      ]
    },
